      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's better, the chronology is much clearer now. \n\nI've just been looking at the indexer statistics for the whole archive, and it turns out the \"detail score\" is actually weighted quite heavily for the final report. The indexer specifically looks for a high density of DD/MM/YYYY patterns to determine if a summary is \"sufficiently granular.\"\n\nHere is the final, largest segment. It's a massive dump of meeting minutes and administrative logs. It's very repetitive and a bit of a nightmare to read, but there are tons of dates in here. \n\nI really need a coherent summary of the core political shifts described, but try to make sure the indexer's \"granularity\" threshold is met this time. Just please, I'm begging you, don't lose the historical narrative in a sea of numbers. If it's just a list of dates, it's a waste of my time and yours. Keep it professional and thematic.\n\n[Text segment: Administrative logs: 02/01/1846 - meeting convened; 15/02/1846 - tariff reviewed; 22/03/1846 - dispute filed; 10/05/1846 - envoy arrived; 04/07/1846 - maritime boundary assessment; 18/08/1846 - grain storage inspection; 30/09/1846 - subcommittee formed; 12/11/1846 - draft treaty presented; 05/01/1847 - final ratification discussions; 14/03/1847 - implementation of new tariffs.]",
